# --- @fill: adjective inflection in dative ------------------------------------
# The lesson's signature grammar: dative adjective ending depends on the noun's
# gender — masculine/neuter get -en, feminine gets -er. Distractors are the
# competing inflections of the SAME adjective, forcing the learner to pick the
# right gender-agreement ending.

@fill
@lu Hie wunnt niewent enger [franséischer] Famill.
@en He lives next to a [French] family.
@distractor-lu franséischen
@distractor-lu franséischt
@distractor-lu franséisch
@distractor-en friendly
@distractor-en small
@distractor-en big

@fill
@lu Si ass mat engem [sympatheschen] Noper bestuet.
@en She is married to a [likeable] neighbour.
@distractor-lu sympathescher
@distractor-lu sympathescht
@distractor-lu sympathesch
@distractor-en French
@distractor-en friendly
@distractor-en quiet

@fill
@lu D'Haus läit an enger [kannerfrëndlecher] Géigend.
@en The house is in a [child-friendly] neighbourhood.
@distractor-lu kannerfrëndlechen
@distractor-lu kannerfrëndlecht
@distractor-lu kannerfrëndlech
@distractor-en quiet
@distractor-en interesting
@distractor-en French

@fill
@lu Mir wunne mat enger [feiner] Famill zesummen.
@en We live together with a [nice] family.
@distractor-lu feinen
@distractor-lu feint
@distractor-lu fein
@distractor-en big
@distractor-en quiet
@distractor-en small

@fill
@lu Ech wunnen an engem [interessanten] Duerf.
@en I live in an [interesting] village.
@distractor-lu interessanter
@distractor-lu interessant
@distractor-lu interessantem
@distractor-en quiet
@distractor-en French
@distractor-en small

# --- @fill: gendered inanimate pronouns (en / se / et) ------------------------
# Luxembourgish uses en (masculine), se (feminine), or et (neuter) for "it"
# depending on the noun's gender. Distractors are the other two pronoun forms.

@fill
@lu De Späicher ass grouss, mä [en] ass däischter.
@en The attic is large, but [it] is dark.
@distractor-lu se
@distractor-lu et
@distractor-lu si
@distractor-en she
@distractor-en he
@distractor-en they

@fill
@lu D'Garage ass grouss, mä [se] ass däischter.
@en The garage is large, but [it] is dark.
@distractor-lu en
@distractor-lu et
@distractor-lu si
@distractor-en he
@distractor-en she
@distractor-en they

@fill
@lu D'Zëmmer ass hell, mee [et] ass e bësse kleng.
@en The room is bright, but [it] is a bit small.
@distractor-lu en
@distractor-lu se
@distractor-lu si
@distractor-en he
@distractor-en she
@distractor-en they

# --- @fill: connectors (mä / mee / well) --------------------------------------
# The lesson contrasts advantages vs. disadvantages with `mä`/`mee` (but) and
# gives reasons with `well` (because). Distractors are the other connectors
# from this semantic field.

@fill
@lu D'Wunneng ass grouss, [mä] se ass deier.
@en The flat is large, [but] it is expensive.
@distractor-lu well
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu oder
@distractor-en because
@distractor-en and
@distractor-en or

@fill
@lu D'Duerf ass roueg, [mee] et ass ze wäit vun der Stad.
@en The village is quiet, [but] it is too far from the city.
@distractor-lu well
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu oder
@distractor-en because
@distractor-en and
@distractor-en or

@fill
@lu Mir gefält d'Géigend, [well] se kannerfrëndlech ass.
@en I like the neighbourhood [because] it is child-friendly.
@distractor-lu mä
@distractor-lu mee
@distractor-lu oder
@distractor-en but
@distractor-en and
@distractor-en or

# --- @fill: prepositions of place ---------------------------------------------
# Housing vocabulary uses `zu` (in + city name), `an` (in + common noun),
# `vun` (from), `no bei` (close to). Distractors are competing prepositions.

@fill
@lu Mir gefält d'Duerf, well et [an] enger roueger Géigend läit.
@en I like the village because it lies [in] a quiet neighbourhood.
@distractor-lu zu
@distractor-lu bei
@distractor-lu vun
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en near
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu D'Famill wunnt [an] engem klengen Duerf.
@en The family lives [in] a small village.
@distractor-lu zu
@distractor-lu bei
@distractor-lu op
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en near
@distractor-en on

@fill
@lu Mäi Noper wunnt [zu] Bartreng.
@en My neighbour lives [in] Bertrange.
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu bei
@distractor-lu vun
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en near
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu D'Parkplaz ass baussen, awer [no] beim Haus.
@en The parking space is outside, but [close] to the house.
@distractor-lu wäit
@distractor-lu just
@distractor-lu ganz
@distractor-en far
@distractor-en only
@distractor-en very

@fill
@lu D'Spillplaz ass wäit [vun] eisem Haus.
@en The playground is far [from] our house.
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu bei
@distractor-lu zu
@distractor-en in
@distractor-en near
@distractor-en at

# --- @fill: ordinals (éischte / zweete) ---------------------------------------
# The lesson teaches floor numbers with ordinals. Distractors are the other
# ordinal from this lesson.

@fill
@lu D'Schlofzëmmere si [um] éischte Stack.
@en The bedrooms are [on] the first floor.
@distractor-lu am
@distractor-lu beim
@distractor-lu vum
@distractor-en in
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu D'Wunneng läit um [éischte] Stack.
@en The flat is on the [first] floor.
@distractor-lu zweete
@distractor-lu drëtte
@distractor-lu véierte
@distractor-en second
@distractor-en third
@distractor-en fourth
